/**
 * Brazil's outline, cut into the four ONS subsystems. Generated — do not edit.
 *
 * This file is 32 kB of path data and nothing else. It is separate from
 * `./brazil-subsystems.ts` for one reason: that module is the part a human
 * reads and argues with — which federal unit is in which subsystem, and what
 * that was checked against — and burying it under four path strings would make
 * the arguable part the part nobody scrolls to.
 *
 * ## Source and licence
 *
 * IBGE, *Malhas Territoriais*, served by the Serviço de Dados API:
 *
 *   GET https://servicodados.ibge.gov.br/api/v3/malhas/paises/BR
 *       ?formato=application/vnd.geo+json&intrarregiao=UF&qualidade=minima
 *
 * Fetched 2026-09-15; 27 features, keyed by `properties.codarea` (the IBGE
 * numeric UF code). IBGE is the Brazilian federal statistics agency and its
 * data is published under the Lei de Acesso à Informação (12.527/2011) as open
 * government data, free to use and redistribute with attribution. It is also
 * the *right* source rather than merely a free one: ONS, ANEEL and CCEE all
 * address federal units by IBGE code, so a boundary drawn here and a subsystem
 * assignment made upstream are talking about the same polygons.
 *
 * ## Projection
 *
 * Equirectangular with a standard parallel at 15° S — the middle of Brazil's
 * latitude span: `x = lon · cos 15°`, `y = −lat`. Not equal-area and not
 * conformal. That is a deliberate choice rather than a shortcut, because
 * nothing on this map encodes a quantity in an area or an angle: the regions
 * are named, filled by a risk *class*, and clicked. An Albers would be more
 * correct about land and would buy the reader nothing they can see. The 15° S
 * parallel keeps the country from looking stretched east-west, which a plain
 * `x = lon` does at these latitudes.
 *
 * ## Simplification, and what it cost
 *
 * The raw `minima` mesh is 98 kB of GeoJSON; at full precision the four path
 * strings would be ~90 kB of JavaScript shipped on a screen that draws the map
 * at 380 px. It is run through a **shared topology** (`topojson-server`
 * quantised to 1e5, then `topojson-simplify` at a weight tolerance of 0.004,
 * then merged per subsystem with `topojson-client`) and emitted at one decimal
 * place. Three consequences worth knowing:
 *
 *  - **18 kB for the four regions, 14 kB for the interior state borders** — a
 *    third of the raw mesh, and the country is still recognisably itself at
 *    380 px: the São Francisco mouth, the Marajó cut, Cabo de Santa Marta and
 *    the Rio Grande do Sul lagoons all survive.
 *  - **No slivers between regions.** Because every state boundary is one arc
 *    in one topology, simplification moves both sides of a shared border
 *    identically and merging erases the interior arcs exactly. The four paths
 *    tile Brazil with no gap and no overlap, which is why the map needs no
 *    outline underneath to hide seams.
 *  - **Islands below ~1.5 px are dropped**, Fernando de Noronha and the
 *    Abrolhos among them. They are invisible at any size this map is drawn at
 *    and each cost a subpath. Noronha is a Sistema Isolado anyway — it belongs
 *    to no subsystem — so its absence is arguably more correct than its
 *    presence would be.
 *
 * The build script is not checked in: it ran once, against an API response
 * that is versioned and dated above, and a generator kept alive to be run
 * again is a generator that will be run against a different response.
 */

